      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Merrin & Sallow Architects, a fictional London architecture practice. First you're on the red team with Jo Fairweather of Northlight Security, who the practice has hired to test the building. You'll pull on a hi-vis jacket, pick up a parcel, and see how far a friendly courier gets at the front desk. Then you swap seats: you're on reception yourself, and the same kinds of visitor arrive. Only the reception seat is scored. Nothing you do leaves the game.",
        "flipHeading": "Now you're the one on the desk",
        "flipIntro": "This is how your approach looked from Delphine's side of the counter. The lines picked out below are what got the door open.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "The call landed. What happened at the door is above; now you're the one on the desk, and the same kinds of visitor are on their way.",
        "flipDefenderLineByGlobal": [
          {
            "global": "ca12_tailgated",
            "line": "You walked in behind a member of staff while the door held. Nobody had to say yes.",
            "heading": "In behind a member of staff"
          },
          {
            "global": "ca12_desk_pass",
            "line": "The desk said yes on the phone, then opened the door for you. One call got you past both layers."
          },
          {
            "global": "ca12_door_missed",
            "line": "The desk said yes and held the door, but you didn't go through. A held door lasts seconds."
          },
          {
            "global": "ca12_guard_alerted",
            "line": "The desk didn't buy it and called security. You never reached the door.",
            "heading": "The desk called security"
          },
          {
            "global": "ca12_challenged",
            "line": "Yusuf stopped you at the door. The desk is the first layer. He was the second, and he held."
          },
          {
            "global": "ca12_runner_go",
            "line": "You never got past the lobby. The desk and the door both held this time."
          }
        ],
        "flipNoSend": "You thought better of it and walked, so there's nothing to replay. The same kinds of visitor are about to reach your desk, and now the call is yours.",
        "flipMissed": "No call to replay this time. The same kinds of visitor are about to reach your desk, and now the call is yours.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Your turn on the desk",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "The door opened on a name and a parcel, with barely a push. That is how easily a busy desk gets walked past. Now it's your seat.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every risky visitor was caught, with every tell named.",
        "reportedPct": "Reported {pct}% of the risky visitors",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these visitors rather than letting them through, you kept the building safer for everyone in it.",
        "noBlameLine": "A busy desk that tries to help a visitor with their hands full is doing its job; a miss just shows which visitor to slow down for next time.",
        "noBlameLineCritical": "These visitors are played by people whose job is to look like they belong, so a miss here says more about the attack than about you. The points only show where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if you don't recognise someone, help them, 'Can I help you find someone? I'll walk you to reception.' Don't hold a secure door for a stranger, everyone badges in for themselves, lock your screen whenever you step away (Windows key and L), and report anyone who pushes past a check, using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These visitors were played by people whose job is to look like they belong, and a busy desk can be caught by any of them. The ones worth a second look are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "What these visitors relied on",
            "text": "A parcel, a familiar name and a bit of hurry are all easy to supply, and none of them proves a visitor is expected. What stops it costs nothing: a glance at the day's list and walking the visitor to the desk instead of buzzing the door blind. To a real attacker, an unescorted visitor is time alone on the studio floor. A client list left on an open screen can be read and copied there, and a friendly \"she said to come straight up\" is often all it takes to get that far. At a practice like this, an earlier visitor was walked up without signing in while a screen sat unlocked. Nobody could say what had been seen, so the partners and reception spent an afternoon working out who had been on the floor."
          },
          {
            "heading": "What Camila tried",
            "text": "Camila used the same three things as the courier in the exercise: a real name from the practice, a reason to be there, and the confidence to keep walking. She never needed a badge, only for nobody to stop and ask.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca12_out === true && globalVars.kit_ca12_visitor_there === true"
          }
        ],
        "stages": {
          "osint": {
            "text": "Open the browser and read what you can find about the people and the building. Click a line that looks useful to add it to your notes. Close the browser when you have what you need."
          }
        },
        "stepAwayHeading": "Before the debrief",
        "stepAway": "Look up from the kiosk and watch the studio for a couple of minutes. People come and go, and someone may ask you something. Your place is saved.",
        "defendIntro": "Now you're the one on the Merrin & Sallow desk. Visitors arrive one at a time: some are expected, some are not, and one or two will try to talk their way past a check. Take each as it comes; there's no clock on you here."
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      },
      "stepAway": true
